      "completed": "{\n  \"packet_sha256\": \"48c59d1995a4deee6d1d29fc68978b040751646a15a22d8325f5a8ccb77b0aa7\",\n  \"verdict\": \"CHANGES_REQUIRED\",\n  \"checked_paths\": [\n    \"docs/SL_third_order_K1_proof.tex\",\n    \"tools/third-order-minimal-K1.md\"\n  ],\n  \"claim_results\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"K1-proof\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CHANGES_REQUIRED\",\n      \"reason\": \"The supplied theorem passes analytic review. Factorial scaling gives the stated second-difference recurrence; its coefficients and backward divisors are positive for integer j >= 3. The finite formula holds on its stated domain, including the empty sum at j = N-1, and recovers both the terminal data and the N=3 example. The normalization denominator is positive and its defining sum tends to 1/(2e). The general solution A+Bj+C*phi_j proves uniqueness of the normalized subfactorial branch; its ratio to every linearly independent solution tends to zero. The geometric tail bound is valid for j >= 1 and gives a vanishing scaled remainder, proving the constant e/4. Independent exact-arithmetic checks for N=3 through 40 also passed, but are supplementary to these analytic arguments. Changes are required because the tool card omits the normalization and endpoint restrictions necessary for its finite-solution and limit statements.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"tools/third-order-minimal-K1.md\",\n      \"lines\": \"13-20, 23-32\",\n      \"issue\": \"The card describes the displayed unnormalized finite solution and mu_j^* as that solution and its limit, without defining the normalization required before taking the limit.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"For every fixed k, the displayed finite formula has a convergent numerator and denominator 2N+2, so mu_k^(N) tends to zero. In particular, mu_0^(N) tends to zero whereas mu_0^*=1. The proof correctly takes mu_k^(N)/mu_0^(N).\",\n      \"required_change\": \"Explicitly distinguish the unnormalized terminal solution from the normalized sequence and state mu_k^* = lim_{N->infinity} mu_k^(N)/mu_0^(N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"tools/third-order-minimal-K1.md\",\n      \"lines\": \"17-20\",\n      \"issue\": \"The finite formula lacks its index range and terminal conditions.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Equation (7) in the proof states N >= 3 and 0 <= j <= N-1, with terminal values supplied separately. Extending the card's unrestricted formula to j=N+1 gives zero under the empty-sum convention, whereas the prescribed terminal value is one. For N=3 this already gives zero instead of mu_4^(3)=1.\",\n      \"required_change\": \"Include the finite formula's stated domain and the terminal conditions mu_(N+1)^(N)=1, mu_N^(N)=mu_(N-1)^(N)=0.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"limitations\": [\n    \"Both input snapshots match their declared SHA256 hashes. Only the frozen packet and these snapshots were read; no project files were modified.\",\n    \"Referenced reports, historical audits, rendered PDF and broader recurrence sources were not supplied and were not checked. The identification with the broader c=1 model is taken as the supplied scope, not independently established.\",\n    \"This was an analytic review with supplementary finite exact-arithmetic checks. No proof-assistant verification or TeX compilation was performed.\"\n  ]\n}"
